.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Peter Debye (1912) explained why some gases have a dielectric constant that falls with temperature: their molecules carry a permanent electric dipole moment \boldsymbol\mu = \sum_i q_i \mathbf r_i, which thermal motion partly disorders. The measured moment tests a proposed shape. Water&#x27;s 1.85 D rules out a linear H-O-H, while carbon dioxide&#x27;s zero moment requires a linear O=C=O.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/structure/dipole/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_debye_dipole_moments_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/structure/dipole/plot_01_debye_dipole_moments`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Debye's permanent dipole moments: polar and nonpolar molecules from geometry</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/structure/dipole/plot_01_debye_dipole_moments`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Arthur Schoenflies named every point group after the symmetry elements that generate it: a principal axis C_n, extra C_2 axes (D_n), mirror planes \sigma_v or \sigma_h, an inversion centre i, and the special cubic groups T_d and O_h. This example builds four molecules from their real 3D geometry, lets determine_point_group find the symmetry elements by testing the coordinates directly, and shows how the elements it finds spell out each Schoenflies symbol: C_{2v}, C_{3v}, T_d and D_{\infty h}.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/structure/point_group/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_point_groups_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/structure/point_group/plot_01_point_groups`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Schoenflies point-group symbols for water, ammonia, methane, and carbon dioxide</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/structure/point_group/plot_01_point_groups`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="In 1893 Alfred Werner proposed that a six-coordinate metal complex places its ligands at the corners of an octahedron. No experiment could see the shape directly, so he argued from isomer counts. A complex \mathrm{MA_4B_2} such as [\mathrm{Co(NH_3)_4Cl_2}]^+ has only two forms (green trans &quot;praseo&quot; and violet cis &quot;violeo&quot;). An octahedron gives exactly two ways to place the two B ligands; a flat hexagon or a trigonal prism gives three. For \mathrm{MA_3B_3} the octahedron again gives two (fac and mer).">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/structure/point_group/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_octahedral_symmetry_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/structure/point_group/plot_02_octahedral_symmetry`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Werner's coordination theory: counting isomers to prove the octahedron</div>
    </div>


